%%%%** Section 1 
\section{The OSI Network Protocol Stack} 
OSI = ``Open Systems Interconnect"  A {\bf model} of network organization in seven layers of abstraction. 

There are two similar modeling systems:  OSI and TCP/IP.   We will use the slightly more general OSI layer to give the overview and then specialize to TCP/IP. 

There is something of a religious war between the two.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


The seven OSI  layers are:
 
\begin{enumerate}
\item Physical Layer
\item Data Link Layer
\item Network Layer
\item Transport Layer
\item Session Layer
\item Presentation Layer
\item Application Layer
\end{enumerate}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.1
\subsection{Physical and Application Layers}

Easiest to understand are (1) and (7):

\begin{itemize}
  \item  (1) Physical Layer:  Cat-5 cable, network jack, ethernet card. 
  \item  (7) Application Layer; Firefox, Chat client, BitTorrent etc.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

What about the others?:

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2
\subsection{ (2) Data Link Layer}

Group bits into Frames and move frames of  data from one node to another across a single physical link.  Detect and correct bit errors in the link. 

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item ethernet
  \item 802.11 wireless
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.3
\subsection{ (3) Network Layer}
End-to-end data delivery.  String together point-to-point links as necessary to get data from one end to another. 

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Address Resolution Prototcol (ARP)
  \item Connectionless Internet Protocol (IP)
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.4
\subsection{ (4) Transport Layer}
Distribute data from the network layer among various applications on the same node.  
Assign socket numbers to each stream so that data is multiplexed on to the network.
Provide end-to-end error checking and re-transmission (optional).   Provide flow control so that links and computers are not overloaded.

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item UDP (used in address resolution protocol)
  \item TCP (used for web browsing, email, chat, etc)
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.5
\subsection{ (5) Session Layer}

Establish semi-permanent dialogs between end-user applications.   Not used by all applications.  Not part of Tcp/Ip model. 

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item  Appletalk
  \item  RPC (Remote Procedure Call)
  \item  SSH/SCP (Secure Shell/Secure Copy) between hosts.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.6
\subsection{ (6) Presentation Layer}
Work on the data itself.

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Encryption/Decryption
  \item Compression / Decompression
  \item Character Set conversion
\end{itemize}



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% 
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Tcp/Ip Internet Model}


Four layers.

There is a {\bf rough} mapping to OSI layers:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Link Layer  (OSI 1,2)
  \item Internet Layer (OSI 3)
  \item Transport Layer (OSI 4) 
  \item Application Layer (OSI 5,6,7)
\end{enumerate}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.1
\subsection{Link Layer}

\begin{itemize}
  \item  Physical layer
  \item  Node to node transmission 
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.2
\subsection{Internet Layer}

\begin{itemize}
  \item  Bridging different (sub)networks
  \item  source computer to destination computer routing
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.3
\subsection{Transport Layer}

\begin{itemize}
  \item End-to-end transfer, identification of ports.
  \item Application to application routing.
  \item Error control / retransmission
  \item Flow control
  \item TCP  connection oriented, reliable packets with re-transmission
  \item UDP  connectionless ``datagram" packets without confirmation. 
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.4
\subsection{Application Layer}
Examples:
\begin{itemize}
  \item HTTP
  \item SMTP (sendmail transfer protocol) for email.
  \item  SSH/SCP (Secure Shell/Secure Copy) between hosts.
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>\includegraphics[height=3.50in]{InternetRouteLayers.eps}
<s>\includegraphics[height=2.2in]{InternetRouteLayers.eps} 

Example of an Internet connection going through the 4-layer  Internetprotocol stack.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 2.5
\subsection{Internet Protocol Packet Types}

Two major types of packets on internet:

\begin{itemize}
  \item  {\bf TCP}    Reliable.   IP Layer 3 will retransmit until valid data is received at final destination.   Slow
  \item  {\bf UDP}    Fast.   IP Layer 3 sends and forgets.  Packets can be lost or arrive at destination out of sequence.    
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}



%%%%** Section 3 
\section{Internet Addressing}

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{Address Format}
An internet protocol address consists of four integers $ 0 <= i <256$.   They are written (for humans) separated by periods:

Example:    {\tt 128.95.42.10} is ``ee.washington.edu".

<!s> Every node on the internet has an IP address.    Each digit of the IP address can be represented by an unsigned 8 bit value.  There are three ranges of IP addresses which can be freely used on {\bf private} networks (such as the one we will have in the lab).  These are:

<s> {\bf Private Address Ranges}


{\tt 10.0.0.0} $\to$ {\tt 10.255.255.255}

{\tt 172.16.0.0} $\to$ {\tt 172.31.255.255}

{\tt 192.168.0.0} $\to$ {\tt 192.168.255.255}
 



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.2
\subsection{How to obtain an address}
There are two ways to get an IP address (such as for a new machine for example):

\begin{itemize}
   \item Get one dynamically (automatically) using a protocol called DHCP. (May change each time you restart).
   \item Get a static (permanent) one assigned to you by the network administrator. 
\end{itemize}


<!s> Servers usually require static IP addresses because users need to know how to connect to them, but clients often get their IP dyanmically assigned through DHCP.  This allows networks to recycle addresses when not in use. 

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.3
\subsection{Ports}

Numbers which identify specific user and server connections within a computer.  

0 $\leftarrow$ 2000          Reserved for system ports

2001 $\leftarrow$ 32767      User ports

Servers bind to a specific port which indicates type of server.   Example:  Http is port 80.

Clients get a random port number assigned to them by OS.   Servers can ``{\tt accept()}" multiple connections on their port.   Clients are one-to-one with port numbers. 


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 4 
\section{EE472 Private Lab Network}

The EE472 lab will have a separate local ethernet which connects all the Make Controllers with two Linux servers.  This net will not be bridged to the Internet or the EE department network.

In the 472 lab, we will assign a static IP to each team for you to program into your Make Controller as follows:


\begin{tabular}{ll}
{\tt 192.168.0.100} $\to$ {\tt 192.168.0.120}    &      Make Controllers \\
{\tt 192.168.0.25} and {\tt 192.168.0.26}       &       Linux servers. 
\end{tabular}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
