%%%%** Section 5 
\section{Sockets}

%%%%** Section 5.1
\subsection{Berkeley Sockets API}
Sockets are an API into the Internet Transport Layer for sending information between applications located on different systems over the network using TCP or UDP.

Originated at U.C. Berkeley in 1980's.   Most common API for networking.   

Windows version:  {\tt winsock}.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Steps to program a connection with sockets:

{\tt \#include <sys/socket.h> }
 
{\bf Server:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Open a ``socket" with   {\tt socket()}.
  \item Server binds the socket to a ``port" identified by an integer using {\tt bind()}
  \item Server makes the socket ``listen" for incoming connections. {\tt listen()}
  \item Accept incoming connection with {\tt accept() }
  \item Send or receive data to/from remote system use {\tt send()/recv() or write()/read()}
  \item Close the socket when done with {\tt close()}
\end{enumerate}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Client:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Create a socket with {\tt socket()}
  \item Connect to a server.  Specify host address and port number with {\tt connect()}
  \item Send or receive data to/from remote system using {\tt send()/recv() or write()/read()}
  \item Close the socket when done with {\tt close()}
\end{enumerate}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%<chn>\includegraphics[width=3.50in]{socket-programs.eps}
%<s>\includegraphics[height=2.75in]{socket-programs.eps} 
